# Organ Trio Sonata No. 4 in E minor, BWV 528: II. Andante (J. S. Bach)
#
# Character: a slow, singing trio in B minor. The two upper lines share
# a subject of falling broken intervals in eighths (F-sharp, B, E,
# C-sharp...) that dissolves into ornamented sixteenths and
# thirty-seconds, over a pedal that moves in detached eighths with
# rests between them. Twice the upper lines hold long notes (half notes
# tied over) while the other line spins figuration: those are the
# expressive heart of the piece.
#
# Time signature: 4/4, 45 bars, no upbeat, no repeats (Mutopia
# SonataIV-1.mid, checked against Andante.ly: it is the second score in
# the set, the Andante; the MIDI holds exactly 45 bars).
#
# Tempo: quarter = 40, about 4:35. Confirmed track times: Ton Koopman
# (Teldec, 1995, Discogs and Amazon listings) 4:38, about quarter = 39;
# George Baker (organ, 1976, Apple Music Classical) 5:29, about 33;
# Roberto Loreggian and Mario Folena (harpsichord and flute arrangement)
# 3:50, about 47. The Mutopia MIDI has eighth = 78, Koopman's speed. 40
# follows Koopman: Andante, a walking pace, not an adagio. On the chip
# the tied half notes (bars 12-13, 19-20, 29-30, 36-37) last over three
# seconds and decay well before they end; that cannot be helped, and a
# slower tempo would make it worse.
#
# Structure (my reading of the score; no published bar plan was found):
#   1-2    subject, soprano, B minor (alto silent).
#   3-4    subject, alto; the soprano goes on in sixteenths.
#   5-6    to F-sharp minor.
#   7-10   the subject in F-sharp minor, soprano (7), then alto (9).
#   11-14  long tied notes in each line in turn over a rising bass;
#          cadence in D major at 14.
#   15-18  over a C-sharp pedal (17-18): dominant of F-sharp minor.
#   19-23  long notes again, to A major (22) and E minor.
#   24-27  the subject in E minor, soprano (24), alto (26).
#   28-31  long notes, cadence in G major at 31.
#   32-35  over an F-sharp pedal (34-35): the dominant of B minor.
#   36-39  long notes, a trill on the alto's falling scale (38-39).
#   40-43  the subject returns in B minor, soprano (40), alto (42).
#   44-45  a pause on E-sharp (fermata), then the close in B minor.
#
# Plan: as in the Vivace, a trio keeps its registration, so the lines
# are balanced equally and the level moves only at the main cadences,
# as an assistant might retire a stop: the pedal-point passages over the
# dominant are softer (they wait), the subject's returns are a step
# louder. Only two levels, mp and p: this is a quiet movement. No `lead`:
# the upper lines are equal partners and cross often. Pedal notes below
# B2 are lifted an octave by the converter.

tempo 40

voice top 0           # soprano and alto equal
voice mid 0
voice bottom 0        # the pedal's detached eighths at full voice

dyn 1 mp              # bars 1-14: exposition, to the D major cadence
broaden 14 0.93       # bar 14: the cadence in D major
dyn 15 p              # bars 15-23: over the C-sharp pedal, and the
                      # long notes that follow
dyn 24 mp             # bars 24-31: the subject in E minor
broaden 31 0.95       # bar 31: cadence in G major
dyn 32 p              # bars 32-39: over the F-sharp pedal, long notes
dyn 40 mp             # bars 40-45: the subject's return in B minor

rit 2 0.65            # bars 44-45: the pause on E-sharp and the close
